WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.890
Risk conditions are configurations or dependencies that weaken expected isolation.

00:00:04.890 --> 00:00:11.056
A privileged container is a major example because it receives broad host-facing authority.

00:00:11.056 --> 00:00:18.923
Privilege should be treated as a documented exception with a narrow purpose, explicit owner, and compensating controls.

00:00:18.923 --> 00:00:24.238
Excessive Linux capabilities create a similar problem at a smaller scale.

00:00:24.238 --> 00:00:28.278
Workloads should receive only the capabilities they require.

00:00:28.278 --> 00:00:36.995
Running as root, permitting privilege escalation, or exposing host devices can further expand the effect of a container compromise.

00:00:36.995 --> 00:00:42.311
HostPath and other host mounts intentionally expose node files or directories.

00:00:42.311 --> 00:00:48.902
The threat model should identify the exact path, access mode, data sensitivity, and reason.

00:00:48.902 --> 00:00:54.643
Broad or writable mounts can undermine filesystem isolation and increase node impact.

00:00:54.643 --> 00:01:02.509
Runtime sockets and management interfaces are highly sensitive because they may control workload creation or host resources.

00:01:02.509 --> 00:01:06.974
They should not be exposed to ordinary application containers.

00:01:06.974 --> 00:01:13.991
Network policy alone does not compensate for a powerful local interface mounted into a workload.

00:01:13.991 --> 00:01:22.283
Weak pod-security settings, unsafe platform defaults, vulnerable images, unnecessary packages, and overly broad Kubernetes permissions all increase risk.

00:01:22.283 --> 00:01:30.575
Broad service-account access can turn a workload issue into orchestration or cluster impact even without direct host control.

00:01:30.575 --> 00:01:32.488
Conditions often combine.

00:01:32.488 --> 00:01:42.907
A vulnerable image running with privilege, a host mount, and broad RBAC has a much larger blast radius than any one finding suggests.

00:01:42.907 --> 00:01:53.325
Review deployments as complete scenarios, enforce policy at admission, inventory exceptions, and prioritize combinations that connect workloads to high-value host or cluster assets.
